                <pb facs="0575" n="1535" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1535"/>

                <head>No. 1041.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Winchester to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berne</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-08-17"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 17, 1888</date>.
                        (Received August 27.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 230.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Last year the congress of the institute of
                    international law took place at Heidelberg, and on the 30th of September next it
                    will be opened at Lausanne, the seat of the Swiss federal tribunal.</p>
                <p>The list of subjects for discussion was selected at last year’s assembly and
                    comprises the common features of the conflict of civil laws; the conflict of the
                    laws relative to marriage and divorce; joint stock companies, and encounters at
                    sea. Then comes the law of extradition, international penal law, the occupation
                    of unclaimed territories according to the treaty of Berlin; the international
                    laws concerning railways, telegraphs, and telephones in time of war; and
                    finally, a question of vital importance for Switzerland, in what manner and in
                    what limits can governments exercise the right of expulsion of strangers.</p>
                <p>I am etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Boyd Winchester.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
